<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Where the Anthropic API key lives. NOT in this file, and not in any file in this repo.
  Deploying this creates an empty, encrypted slot. A human fills it in Setup, once.

  WHY THE PROTOCOL IS "Password" AND NOT "Custom", which is what you would reach for first.
  The org was asked directly. Its ExternalCredential.AuthenticationProtocol picklist offers
  NoAuthentication, Oauth, Password, AwsSv4, Jwt, JwtExchange, Custom, Basic. "Custom" reads
  like the right answer for an API key in a header, and it is not: it accepts a principal
  but rejects every parameter type that could hold a secret, verbatim from the deploy —

      The authentication protocol "Custom" doesn't support the following external
      credential parameter type(s): CustomPrincipal, AuthParameter.

  So Custom is for protocols implemented in Apex, with the secret held somewhere else. That
  is worse, not better: it puts the key back within reach of Apex.

  "Password" is not available either, despite appearing in that picklist: External
  Credentials reject it outright. That leaves "Basic", which is what this uses.

  Basic is not being used as Basic auth. It is being used for its storage: it is the
  platform's encrypted, write-only secret slot, and the only supported protocol that has
  one. The key goes in the Password field. It is encrypted at rest, cannot be read back
  once saved, and Apex has no access to it. The Authorization header Basic would normally
  generate is switched OFF on the Named Credential (generateAuthorizationHeader false);
  instead the Named Credential merges the same stored secret into x-api-key, which is the
  header Anthropic actually authenticates on. The merge happens after Apex has finished
  building the request, so a debug log shows the merge field and not the key.

  The Username field is required by the protocol and unused by Anthropic. Set it to the
  literal "anthropic" so a later reader can tell it is deliberate rather than a leftover.
-->
<ExternalCredential xmlns="http://soap.sforce.com/2006/04/metadata">
    <authenticationProtocol>Basic</authenticationProtocol>
    <description>Anthropic API key. Stored in the encrypted password slot of the named principal, entered in Setup, never in source.</description>
    <externalCredentialParameters>
        <parameterName>AAO_Anthropic_Principal</parameterName>
        <parameterType>NamedPrincipal</parameterType>
        <sequenceNumber>1</sequenceNumber>
    </externalCredentialParameters>
    <label>AAO Anthropic</label>
</ExternalCredential>
